from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(10,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("acoustics","Meena shouted in an empty hall and heard her voice come back. \"Sound reflects,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Sound waves are longitudinal waves. Wave velocity is the distance the wave travels in unit time, and V = n x wavelength, where n is the frequency. Sound travels fastest in solids, slower in liquids and slowest in gases, because gases are the least elastic.",
  "Sound reflects from a surface following the laws of reflection: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection. An echo is heard when the reflected sound reaches the ear at least 0.1 second after the original, so the reflecting surface must be at least about 17 m away. Echoes are used to measure the depth of the sea and the speed of sound. The ear trumpet, megaphone and sound board use reflection.",
  "The Doppler effect is the apparent change in the frequency of sound due to relative motion between the source and the listener. The pitch rises as the source approaches and falls as it moves away. It is used in radar, medical scans and speed guns."],
 ["wave velocity","reflection of sound","echo","sound board","Doppler effect","pitch"],
 ("A sound of frequency 500 Hz has wavelength 0.68 m. Find its speed.",["V = n x wavelength.","500 x 0.68 = 340.","Speed = 340 m/s."]),
 ("The Doppler effect changes the speed of sound.",["It changes the apparent frequency, not the speed.","Relative motion causes the change."]),
 ["V = n x wavelength.","Echo needs at least 0.1 s gap.","Doppler effect changes apparent pitch.","Sound is fastest in solids."],
 ["m|Sound is fastest in|solids|gases|vacuum|Solids are most elastic.","t|An echo needs a reflecting surface.|T|Yes.","m|Apparent change of frequency due to motion is the|Doppler effect|echo|resonance|It changes pitch.","t|Sound travels in a vacuum.|F|It needs a medium.","m|The formula for wave velocity is|V = n x wavelength|V = n/wavelength|V = wavelength/n|Frequency times wavelength.","s|What instrument directs sound using reflection and a cone? Two words.|mega phone;megaphone|A megaphone.","m|The minimum time gap to hear an echo is|0.1 s|1 s|10 s|About 17 m distance.","t|Echoes measure sea depth.|T|By timing a signal."])
C("nuclear-physics","Raju heard that the Sun makes its energy by fusing hydrogen. \"Nuclear energy powers the stars,\" said his teacher.",
 ["Radioactivity is the spontaneous emission of radiation from certain elements, discovered by Becquerel in pitchblende, an ore of uranium. Natural radioactivity occurs on its own and artificial or induced radioactivity makes even light elements radioactive. The disintegrating nucleus is the parent and the one produced is the daughter. The curie, 3.7 x 10^10 disintegrations per second, and the becquerel are the units.",
  "Alpha, beta and gamma are three kinds of radiations and each decay follows displacement laws. Nuclear fission is the splitting of a heavy nucleus like uranium-235 into smaller nuclei with a large release of energy and a chain reaction; the minimum mass needed is the critical mass, and the atom bomb uses an uncontrolled chain. Nuclear fusion is the joining of light nuclei to form a heavier one, as in the Sun and stars, and the hydrogen bomb.",
  "Radioisotopes are used in agriculture, medicine and industry, but radiation is dangerous, so safety measures and permitted limits are followed. A nuclear reactor produces controlled fission energy, and India has several nuclear power plants."],
 ["radioactivity","alpha beta gamma","nuclear fission","chain reaction","nuclear fusion","nuclear reactor"],
 ("Name the energy source of the Sun.",["The Sun combines light nuclei.","This is nuclear fusion.","Hydrogen fuses into helium."]),
 ("Fission joins two light nuclei.",["Fission splits a heavy nucleus.","Fusion joins light nuclei."]),
 ["Radioactivity is spontaneous emission.","Fission splits, fusion joins.","The curie is a unit of radioactivity.","Reactors control chain reactions."],
 ["m|Spontaneous emission of radiation is|radioactivity|fission|fusion|Discovered by Becquerel.","t|Fusion takes place in the Sun.|T|Yes.","m|Splitting a heavy nucleus is|fission|fusion|reflection|Releases energy.","t|Alpha, beta and gamma are types of radiation.|T|Yes.","m|The traditional unit of radioactivity is the|curie|ohm|pascal|3.7 x 10^10 disintegrations per second.","s|What is the minimum mass for a chain reaction called?|critical mass|Critical mass.","m|A nuclear reactor gives|controlled energy|no energy|sound only|It controls the chain reaction.","t|Radiation exposure should follow safety limits.|T|It can harm health."])
C("atoms-and-molecules","Asha counted dozens of bananas and said, \"Chemists count atoms in moles, just like we count in dozens.\"",
 ["Atoms are very small, so we compare their masses with the carbon-12 atom. Relative atomic mass is the ratio of the average mass of an atom of an element to one-twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom, and the average atomic mass is the weighted average over natural isotopes. A molecule is the smallest particle of a substance that can exist freely, and the relative molecular mass is the sum of the atomic masses.",
  "One mole of any substance contains 6.023 x 10^23 particles, which is Avogadro's number, and its mass in grams is the gram atomic mass or gram molecular mass. The number of moles = mass / molar mass. Percent composition tells the percentage by mass of each element in a compound.",
  "Avogadro's hypothesis states that equal volumes of all gases under the same conditions of temperature and pressure contain the same number of molecules. Vapour density is related to relative molecular mass by molecular mass = 2 x vapour density."],
 ["relative atomic mass","molecular mass","mole","Avogadro's number","percent composition","Avogadro's hypothesis"],
 ("How many moles are in 36 g of water (molar mass 18 g)?",["Moles = mass / molar mass.","36 / 18 = 2.","So 2 moles."]),
 ("One mole of oxygen and one mole of hydrogen contain different numbers of molecules.",["One mole of any substance has 6.023 x 10^23 particles.","So the number is the same."]),
 ["A mole has 6.023 x 10^23 particles.","Moles = mass / molar mass.","Equal volumes of gases have equal molecules.","Molecular mass = 2 x vapour density."],
 ["m|The number of particles in a mole is|6.023 x 10^23|3 x 10^8|9.8|Avogadro's number.","t|Atomic mass is compared with carbon-12.|T|Yes.","m|Moles in 36 g of water are|2|3|18|36 / 18.","t|One mole has the same number of particles in every substance.|T|Yes.","m|Percent composition gives percentage of|each element|only oxygen|volume|By mass.","s|Whose hypothesis states equal volumes have equal molecules?|Avogadro|Avogadro.","m|Molecular mass is twice the|vapour density|density|volume|M = 2 x VD.","t|Gram molecular mass is the mass of one mole.|T|Yes."])
C("periodic-classification-of-elements","Meena saw the periodic table on the lab wall. \"Elements in the same column behave alike,\" said her teacher.",
 ["The Modern Periodic Law says that the physical and chemical properties of elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers. The table has seven periods, which are horizontal rows, and eighteen groups, which are vertical columns, and elements in a group have similar properties because they have the same number of outer electrons.",
  "Periodic trends are patterns across a period and down a group. Atomic radius decreases across a period and increases down a group. Ionisation enthalpy, the energy to remove an electron, generally rises across a period and falls down a group, and electronegativity follows a similar pattern.",
  "Metals lie on the left and centre, non-metals on the right and metalloids between them. Understanding periodic trends helps us predict reactions and uses of elements."],
 ["modern periodic law","period","group","atomic radius","ionisation enthalpy","electronegativity"],
 ("Does atomic radius increase or decrease across a period?",["Across a period the nuclear charge increases.","Electrons are pulled in closer.","So atomic radius decreases."]),
 ("Elements in a period have similar properties.",["Elements in a group have similar properties.","They have the same outer electrons."]),
 ["Properties repeat with atomic number.","Atomic radius falls across a period.","Ionisation enthalpy rises across a period.","Groups have similar properties."],
 ["m|Horizontal rows are called|periods|groups|blocks|There are seven.","t|Atomic radius decreases across a period.|T|Yes.","m|The table is arranged by|atomic number|mass number|density|Modern law.","t|Metals are on the right of the table.|F|Non-metals are on the right.","m|Energy to remove an electron is|ionisation enthalpy|electron affinity|radius|It rises across a period.","s|How many groups does the modern table have?|18;eighteen|Eighteen.","m|Elements in a group have the same number of|outer electrons|neutrons|periods|That gives similar properties.","t|Atomic radius increases down a group.|T|More shells are added."])
